deep.reconstruction:
Context: the notes set up a combinatorial description of the boundary ('structure à l'infini') of M_{g,ν}. The previous page defined a 'standard curve' over a base S by gluing data: (a) Y proper and smooth over S of relative dimension 1; (b) Ã and I, disjoint closed subschemes of Y, finite étale over S; (c) σ_Ã, a free involution of Ã. One forms X̂ = Y/σ, an equisingular nodal family whose nodes are A = Ã/σ, and X = X̂ \ I. Grothendieck had claimed the functor (6), (Y,Ã,I,σ) ↦ X, is fully faithful, then crossed this out as 'faux tel quel!'.

This page does three things.

1. Definition. A relative S-scheme is a 'standard curve' on S if it lies in the essential image of the functor. The datum (Y,Ã,I,σ) producing it is called its 'normalisé' (relative normalization). He flags this as a 'gros abus de langage', since the datum is not just the scheme Y but Y with the extra structure Ã, I, σ_Ã. The crossed-out alternative name was 'système divisant'.

2. Marginal correction. The functor is fully faithful over a field, or better over a reduced S. It fails over an arbitrary S when I ≠ ∅. To get full faithfulness one should work in the proper setting, replacing X by the pair (X̂, I), so that X = X̂ − I.

The mathematical reason: over a non-reduced base such as the dual numbers, X = X̂ − I is affine near the punctures. It therefore has infinitesimal automorphisms id + εD, where D is a vector field with poles along I, and these do not extend to X̂. Over a reduced base, isomorphisms of punctured smooth curves extend uniquely to the smooth compactifications, so fullness holds.

3. Section 2, 'Graphe associé à une courbe standard'. Over an algebraically closed field k, set S = π0(Y), which is in bijection with the irreducible components of X. One gets canonical maps of finite sets: Ã → S (called σ), I → S (called p), and the degree-2 quotient q: Ã → A = Ã/σ_Ã. The next page reads this as a graph in Serre's formalism:
- vertices S = components;
- oriented arcs Ã = branches at the nodes, i.e. half-edges;
- the free involution σ_Ã reverses orientation;
- σ is the origin map;
- edges A = nodes;
- I → S is the extra datum of punctures, i.e. legs/tails.

This is the dual graph (stable graph with legs) of a pointed nodal curve. It serves to index the boundary strata of the Deligne–Mumford–Knudsen compactification of M_{g,ν}.
